From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Abe Massry <amassry@linode.com>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 17:34:27 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 2/8] tcg/wasm32: add a WebAssembly backend for WASI hosts

Add a TCG backend that turns each translation block into a small
WebAssembly module.  It is based on Kohei Tokunaga's Emscripten
backend, "TCG: add wasm backend"
(https://github.com/qemu/qemu/commit/bf5378b605d46548c0387e925fc9683903580487),
whose Emscripten code paths are kept; this patch ports it to WASI, and
fixes and extends it.

Emscripten compiles TB modules through JavaScript.  A WASI module has
no such API, so the host provides it as imports from "env"
(tcg/wasi_jit.h):
- wasi_jit_compile(): compile and instantiate a TB module.  TB modules
  import QEMU's linear memory and function table and call helpers with
  call_indirect.  The host puts the TB's entry function into QEMU's
  table and returns its index, so QEMU runs a TB with an ordinary
  indirect call, and a helper's longjmp unwinds straight through it.
- wasi_jit_load_cached(): the same, but only if the host still has an
  identical module compiled, e.g. from before a TB buffer flush.
- wasi_jit_load_async(): the same, but a module not compiled yet is
  compiled on another host thread, while QEMU keeps interpreting the TB.
- wasi_jit_drop(): QEMU no longer uses a TB's handle.

Execution (tcg/wasm32.c):
- An interpreter tier (tcg/wasm32/wasi-interp.c.inc).  Compiling a
  module costs about 1 ms, so TBs are first interpreted from their
  wasm code, and compiled once they have run QEMU_JIT_HOT times
  (default 5000), in the background (QEMU_JIT_ASYNC=0: synchronously).
  A TB whose module the host still has returns to compiled code after
  QEMU_JIT_WARM runs (default 50).
  wasi-interp-calls.c.inc is generated by qemu-wasi's
  scripts/gen_interp_calls.py.
- TBs chain directly through goto_tb, and their code is position
  independent.  With no thread to kick it, the vCPU returns to the
  main loop every 30 ms (QEMU_TB_SLICE_MS); see accel/tcg.
- tcg/region.c drops the host's modules when the TB buffer is flushed.
- Debugging: QEMU_PROF_EVERY=<secs> prints where the time goes, and
  QEMU_TB_DUMP=<pc>,<dir> saves the module of the TB at <pc>.

On WASI, a TB always runs from its start to an exit: a helper returns
or throws.  So TCG's registers are locals of the TB's function, which
the host compiler can keep in machine registers, rather than module
globals, and helper calls and guest memory accesses need no resume
points.  (Emscripten's coroutines can unwind out of a TB and re-enter
it, which is what the globals and resume points are for there.)

Code generation fixes include wasm block indices, Int128 helper
arguments and results, the upper bits of 32-bit operations (clz, ctz,
extract, deposit, mul2, shifts, division, ctpop), byte swaps, and the
carries of add2/sub2.
